# Gherkin style for business requirements

Requirements are read by product owners, testers, support and developers who never saw the
request. Write declarative Gherkin: what the business needs to be true, never how someone operates
a UI or how the system is built. The test for every line: would it change if the screens, the
technology or the request changed? If yes, rewrite it. The blocks are documentation, not
executable `.feature` files; tests link to scenarios by ID.

Write rule text and glossary in the language the existing requirements use (English by default).
Keywords stay in English so that searches work.

## Keywords

| Keyword | Use | Limits |
|---|---|---|
| Feature | not written; the file's H1 and purpose paragraph play this role | one feature per file |
| Rule | one business rule, stated as a sentence that can be true or false | `Rule: CHK-7 <statement>`; up to 3 lines of description below it, saying why the rule exists |
| Scenario | one concrete example of the rule (`Example` is the same keyword; write `Scenario`) | `Scenario: CHK-7.2 <outcome>`; 3 to 5 steps |
| Scenario Outline + Examples | business variations of one behavior: thresholds, categories, roles | one ID for all rows; at most about 6 rows, each a case the business cares about |
| Background | Given steps shared by every scenario of one Rule | only inside a Rule, at most 3 Givens |
| Given / When / Then | the situation / the one event / the observable outcome | exactly one When step and one Then step |
| And / But | continue the step above with the same meaning | not after When |
| Data table | a short list of business values, such as a fee schedule | no incidental columns |

Not used: doc strings (state what a message means, not its wording, unless the exact wording is a
business or legal requirement), comments, the asterisk step and tags (IDs replace tags).
Indentation: Rule at column 0, Background and Scenario 2 spaces, steps and Examples 4, table rows 6.

## Rule checklist

- [ ] One business rule; the name is a statement ("An Order can be placed only for a Cart with at
  least one Item"), not a topic ("Cart validation").
- [ ] At least one Scenario; a rule that rejects, forbids or limits something also has a negative
  scenario showing what happens when it is broken.
- [ ] Limits get boundary scenarios: at the limit and just past it.
- [ ] A rule of another feature is cited by ID (`Related rules:` line), never copied. Access
  control, audit and notifications are features of their own that others cite.

## Scenario checklist

- [ ] Name: the ID, then the outcome in a phrase; unique within the file.
- [ ] One behavior: one When, one Then (plus And/But lines), 3 to 5 steps, Background excluded.
- [ ] Actors are glossary roles in the third person: "the Customer", never "I", "we" or "the
  user" (unless User is a glossary term).
- [ ] Domain terms are capitalised and defined in the glossary; one word per concept (the
  glossary's Avoid column lists the synonyms not to use).
- [ ] Values appear only when the rule depends on them (limits, amounts written as "50 USD",
  dates); no incidental data such as names, emails or passwords.
- [ ] Then states an observable business outcome (an Order exists, the Customer is told something,
  no Refund is issued), never internal state ("is saved", "the flag is set").
- [ ] Errors are business outcomes: "no Order is placed" and "the Customer is told that the Cart is
  empty", not "an error 400 is returned" or "a message appears under the field".
- [ ] Business time limits are allowed ("within 14 days", "by the end of the Business Day");
  technical metrics (response times, availability percentages) belong to the tech design.
- [ ] Timeless present tense: no story narrative ("As a / I want / So that"), no request or ticket
  references, no change-relative words.

## Flagged vocabulary

| Group | Words | Write instead |
|---|---|---|
| UI | page, screen, button, click, modal, field, form | the intent: "the Customer confirms the Order", "the Customer is told" |
| Technical | API, endpoint, database, table, JSON, HTTP, cache, queue, service, null | the business outcome: "the Order is kept", "the Supplier is notified", "has no Delivery Date" |
| Change-relative | new, now, no longer, currently, will, still, anymore | the timeless present: "is", "can", "cannot" |

A word the glossary defines as a domain term is exempt: "Customer Service", "Call Queue", "Tax
Form", a restaurant's "Table". Every other hit is a warning the user reviews at the gate, not an
automatic failure: forced synonyms make rules harder to read. To find hits, search every edited
file, for example:

```sh
grep -nwiE 'page|screen|button|click|modal|field|form|API|endpoint|database|table|JSON|HTTP|cache|queue|service|null' <file>
grep -nwiE 'new|now|no longer|currently|will|still|anymore' <file>
```

## Bad and good

Imperative UI script (no ID, first person, incidental data, UI and HTTP words, two behaviors):

```gherkin
Scenario: Checkout error
  Given I am logged in as "bob.smith"
  And I am on the cart page
  When I click the "Place order" button
  Then the API returns 400
  And a modal shows "Your cart is empty"
  When I add an item
  Then the button is enabled
```

The same behavior as a business rule:

```gherkin
Rule: CHK-1 An Order can be placed only for a Cart with at least one Item
  Scenario: CHK-1.1 A Customer places an Order for a Cart with Items
    Given a Customer has a Cart with 2 Items
    When the Customer places an Order
    Then an Order with the 2 Items is placed
  Scenario: CHK-1.2 A Customer cannot place an Order for an empty Cart
    Given a Customer has an empty Cart
    When the Customer tries to place an Order
    Then no Order is placed
    And the Customer is told that the Cart is empty
```

A business variation as an outline, with a Background inside its Rule:

```gherkin
Rule: CHK-4 Shipping is free for Orders of at least 50 USD
  Background:
    Given a Customer has a Cart with a Delivery Address in the Home Market
  Scenario Outline: CHK-4.1 The Shipping Fee depends on the Order Total
    Given the Order Total of the Cart is <total>
    When the Customer places an Order
    Then the Shipping Fee of the Order is <fee>
    Examples:
      | total     | fee      |
      | 49.99 USD | 4.90 USD |
      | 50.00 USD | 0 USD    |
```

More fixes:

| Bad | Good | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Then the Order is placed within 200 ms | Then the Order is placed | the metric belongs to the tech design |
| Then the Customer can now see the Refund | Then the Customer can see the Refund | change-relative word |
| Then the order status column is set to CANCELLED | Then the Order is Cancelled | internal state; Cancelled is a glossary state |
| When the Customer submits the return form | When the Customer requests a Return | UI word |
| When the Clerk clicks Export to CSV | When the Clerk exports the Orders of a Business Day | solution wording from the request; the file format belongs to the tech design |
